Go code adapted inside tlsx's own files
---------------------------------------

Some of tlsx's own files contain passages adapted from Go. Each is noted
where it occurs, and each such file has the header line "Portions Copyright
The Go Authors (BSD-3-Clause; see NOTICE)":

- x_renegotiation.go: handleHelloRequest, from (*Conn).handleRenegotiation
  in src/crypto/tls/conn.go.
- x_suites.go: dheKeyAgreement, following ecdheKeyAgreement in
  src/crypto/tls/key_agreement.go; aeadAESCCMTLS13 and aeadAESCCM8TLS13,
  from aeadAESGCMTLS13 in src/crypto/tls/cipher_suites.go.
- x_psk.go: aeadAESCCM12, from aeadAESGCM in src/crypto/tls/cipher_suites.go;
  the TLS 1.2 PSK handshakes, which follow and reuse steps of doFullHandshake
  in src/crypto/tls/handshake_server.go and handshake_client.go and the curve
  checks of processServerKeyExchange in key_agreement.go; loadExternalPSK,
  from loadSession in handshake_client.go; checkExternalPSK, from
  checkForResumption in handshake_server_tls13.go.
- x_messages.go: the certificate_authorities encoding, from
  certificateRequestMsgTLS13, and certificateStatusExt's marshal and
  unmarshal, from certificateStatusMsg, in src/crypto/tls/handshake_messages.go.
- x_rpk.go: the missing-certificate and RSA key size checks, from
  processCertsFromClient in src/crypto/tls/handshake_server.go; the
  VerifyConnection step, from verifyServerCertificate in handshake_client.go;
  the raw-key Certificate framing, from certificateMsg in
  handshake_messages.go.
- x_conn.go: SendKeyUpdate's send steps, from the send half of
  handleKeyUpdate in src/crypto/tls/conn.go.
- x_handshake.go: saveSessionID, from saveSessionTicket in
  src/crypto/tls/handshake_client.go.
- x_psk_resume.go: loadPSKSessionX, from the TLS 1.2 steps of loadSession in
  src/crypto/tls/handshake_client.go.
- x_certs.go and internal/brainpool/encoding.go: ASN.1 structures as in
  src/crypto/x509 (pkcs8.go, sec1.go, x509.go: publicKeyInfo); x_certs.go
  also reuses the error text "tls: failed to parse private key" of
  src/crypto/tls/tls.go.
- internal/brainpool/ecdsa.go: encodeSignature, addASN1IntBytes and
  parseSignature, from src/crypto/ecdsa/ecdsa.go.
- internal/brainpool/curve.go: the elliptic.Curve methods, modelled on
  nistCurve in src/crypto/elliptic/nistec.go.
- internal/tls13/x_external.go: mirrors ResumptionBinderKey in tls13.go.
- internal/ccm/ccm_test.go: unhex, like decodeHex in
  src/crypto/cipher/gcm_test.go.

These passages are Copyright The Go Authors under the BSD-3-Clause licence.
The rest of tlsx's own code follows the structure and idioms of the
surrounding Go code; a line-run comparison of tlsx's own files with Go's
crypto sources (2026-09-28) found no other shared passages beyond generic
test helpers and published OID values.

Published values and test vectors
---------------------------------

tlsx uses values and test vectors published in standards, cited where used:
RFC 3610 section 8 (AES-CCM packet vectors, internal/ccm/testdata),
RFC 5639 (brainpool curve parameters), RFC 7027 Appendix A (brainpool ECDH
vectors), RFC 7919 Appendix A (finite field Diffie-Hellman groups), and
algorithm identifiers from RFC 4055, RFC 5208, RFC 5280, RFC 5915 and
RFC 8446. The revocation package uses the CRL extension identifiers and
reason code names of RFC 5280. No standards text is reproduced. One expected CCM value in
internal/ccm/ccm_test.go was computed with the Python "cryptography"
package's AESCCM as an independent implementation.

internal/bigmod/testdata/mod_inv_tests.txt is distributed by Go under Go's
licence; its format matches BoringSSL's bignum test files and it may
originate there (not verified).

Other third-party material
--------------------------

None known. No code from uTLS, BoringSSL, OpenSSL, GnuTLS, Wycheproof or
other projects is included. OpenSSL and GnuTLS are used only as external
programs by the interoperability tests.

BoringSSL's BoGo test runner (ssl/test/runner; BoringSSL's licence is in
its own LICENSE file) is not included either: scripts/bogo.sh and
TestBogoSuite download BoringSSL at the revision bogo_shim_test.go pins
(through the Go module proxy or git) and run the runner from there.
x_bogo_test.go uses the runner's shim flag names, which are an interface,
and no BoringSSL code.

